  <!-- ===== Generic Lyocell vs TENCEL Lyocell ===== -->
  <h2>Generic Lyocell vs. TENCEL™ Lyocell</h2>
  <p><strong>Generic Lyocell</strong> is a category of regenerated cellulose fibre produced through a direct solvent-spinning process. It can be manufactured by different companies using different production technologies and sourcing practices.</p>
  <p><strong>TENCEL™ Lyocell</strong>, on the other hand, is Lenzing's branded version of Lyocell. It is produced using Lenzing's specific manufacturing technologies, wood-sourcing requirements and production standards.</p>
  <p>The main difference is not necessarily the basic fibre chemistry, but the manufacturer, production system, sourcing controls and documented product specifications.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Comparison</th><th>Generic Lyocell</th><th>TENCEL™ Lyocell</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Name</td><td>Generic fibre category</td><td>Registered brand</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manufacturer</td><td>Different manufacturers</td><td>Lenzing AG</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Raw material</td><td>Usually dissolving wood pulp</td><td>Certified or controlled wood sources</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Production</td><td>Lyocell solvent-spinning process</td><td>Lenzing's specific production technology</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Solvent recovery</td><td>Depends on manufacturer</td><td>Lenzing reports over 99.8% recovery</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Traceability</td><td>Depends on producer</td><td>Lenzing provides fibre identification and traceability solutions</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p><strong>An important point</strong> is that generic Lyocell is not necessarily environmentally harmful or lower quality. Its environmental performance depends on the individual manufacturer's production process and raw-material sourcing.</p>
  <p>Lenzing claims that its TENCEL™ Lyocell fibres have at least 50% lower carbon emissions and water consumption compared with generic Lyocell. This is a company-reported comparison, so its methodology and system boundaries should be checked before using it as an independent conclusion.</p>

