  <!-- ===== Lyocell and Modal properties ===== -->
  <h2>Lyocell and Modal: regenerated cellulose fibres</h2>
  <p>Lyocell and Modal are both regenerated cellulose fibres. This means they are manufactured by dissolving cellulose obtained from plant-based materials and regenerating it into textile fibres. However, they differ in their manufacturing processes and physical properties.</p>

  <!-- A. Lyocell -->
  <h3>A. Lyocell</h3>
  <p>Lyocell is produced by directly dissolving cellulose in an organic solvent, commonly N-Methylmorpholine-N-oxide (NMMO). The dissolved cellulose is then passed through a spinneret to form fibres.</p>
  <p><strong>Main characteristics:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>High tensile strength, including when wet.</li>
    <li>Soft and smooth texture.</li>
    <li>Good moisture absorption.</li>
    <li>Suitable for clothing, denim and home textiles.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- B. Modal -->
  <h3>B. Modal</h3>
  <p>Modal is a type of regenerated cellulose fibre generally manufactured through a modified viscose process. It is designed to provide high breaking strength and high wet modulus, meaning it maintains its strength and dimensional stability relatively well when wet.</p>
  <p><strong>Main characteristics:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Excellent softness.</li>
    <li>Good moisture absorption.</li>
    <li>Good dimensional stability.</li>
    <li>Maintains softness after repeated washing.</li>
    <li>Commonly used in underwear, sportswear and everyday clothing.</li>
  </ul>

